    "question": {
      ":question/id": "399bf382-5842-55a7-b0e1-559f53fd4313",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{3}^{3x}}{{y}^{4}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={3}^{3x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={3}^{3x}\n$$",
